  "content": "Rs 2.5 billion limit on share loans removed, banks allowed easier trading\nOnlinekhabar\nThursday, October 9, 2025\n0\nComments\nPhoto by Nimisha Mekala on Unsplash\nKathmandu, October 9\nNepal Rastra Bank (NRB) has lifted the Rs 2.5 billion ceiling on share loans that banks could issue to individuals. The central bank announced the change through a new directive to banks and financial institutions.\nThe decision follows the implementation of recommendations made by the Capital Market Reform Task Force formed by former Finance Secretary Rameshwar Khanal. The directive also makes it easier for banks to engage in share trading.\nPreviously, banks were allowed to sell only up to 20 per cent of their primary capital investment in shares that had been held for more than one fiscal year. This restriction has now been removed.\nSimilarly, banks can now invest in the shares and debentures of listed companies for a period exceeding six months. Earlier, such investments were permitted only for durations longer than one year.\nReact to this post\nHot Topics\nSushila Karki\nConversation\nNew\nOld\nPopular\nAdd Comment\nLogin to comment\nRemenber me\nForgot password?\nLogin\nNot have account yet?\nSignup Now\nOr use Social Media?\nGoogle\nFacebook\nTwitter\nForgot Password?\nSend Password Reset Link\nBack\nRelated News\nNEPSE: Stock market at 26-month high reaching 2257 points\n1 year ago\nNEPSE drops by 9.32 points, trading volume nears Rs 6.5 billion\n1 year ago\nNEPSE: Business volume increases though share index declines\n1 year ago\nStock market declines by 1.03% on Sunday, NEPSE Falls to 2109 Points\n1 year ago\nNEPSE: The stock market exceeded 2,000 points\n1 year ago\nNEPSE: Finance gained 4 % in the stock market\n1 year ago\nMost Read\nUAE introduces new rules for Visit Visa Sponsorship\n12 stranded backpackers rescued from Langtang\nGold price reaches record high at Rs 235,960 per tola\nWhen a joke fuels division: Casual hate speech and the Gen Z revolution in Nepal\nGold price surges by Rs 10,000 per tola after Dashain\nSubscribe to our newsletter\nSubscribe to Onlinekhabar English to get notified of exclusive news stories.\nEditor's Pick\nEditor's Pick\n4 government buildings, including Supreme Court, declared unfit for use\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nCharred body found at Koteshwor Bhatbhateni, Gen-Z protest death toll rises to 74\n4 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nNo consensus on Hami Nepal’s claim of leading the Gen-Z movement\n4 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nDecades-old irrigation projects stall as Madhesh faces drought, food crisis looms\n3 months ago\nEditor's Pick\nNepal’s farming crisis: When plans stay on paper and fields suffer\n3 months ago\nAlso Read\nOpinion\nThe rise of Nepal’s first woman Prime Minister\n41 minutes ago\nPolitics\nWho do student unions stand for? Questions rise after Gen Z Movement\n2 days ago\nEconomy\nThai AirAsia to resume flights from Gautam Buddha International Airport\n2 days ago\nFestival\nFlowers ready for Tihar Festival (Photos)\n2 days ago\nOpinion\nGen Z movement in Nepal at a crossroads: Between reform, revolt and reflection\n3 days ago",
